Ours (PhoneCanDo), not from llama.cpp. When a chat template refuses the
messages as sent, makes them plain and renders once more: tool references
become text and the system messages are folded, so a strict template takes
what agents send.

Codex (Responses API) starts with two developer messages, which the server
maps to system, and Claude Code (Anthropic /v1/messages) sends its
environment block as a system message after the user turn. Qwen3.5's
template allows one system message, first, and raises "System message must
be at the beginning" otherwise, so every request from either failed.
Claude Code's tool search also answers with tool_reference parts (the
deferred tools it found), which Qwen3.5's template refuses ("Unexpected
item type in content."), so a turn failed as soon as the model searched.

common_chat_templates_apply, which chat completions, Responses and
Anthropic messages all reach, renders the messages as sent first: a
template that takes them (Gemma 4, Llama 3.x, Qwen3, GLM, Mistral, LFM3
with its tool references and others) renders exactly as before. Only if
that throws (the template's raise arrives wrapped differently per path, so
any error counts) does it render once more with the messages made plain,
after developer is mapped to system; that attempt's error, if any, is the
one reported, and everything it returns (prompt, grammar, parser) comes
from the attempt that rendered.

Tool references: a run of them becomes one text part, "Tool loaded:
<name>" a line per tool, on lines of its own. A template that refuses
them (Qwen3.5's) also lists every tool up front, deferred or not, so the
line says all the reference did.

The fold:
- the leading run of system messages becomes one, their text a blank line
  apart;
- a later one becomes "[System note]\n<text>": at the end of the user or
  tool message before it, else at the start of the user message after it,
  else (after an assistant turn) a user message of its own;
- in text parts the text joins the part next to it (templates such as
  Gemma 4's trim each part); other parts stay as they are. Text is trimmed
  first, and blank system messages are dropped.

